    "q-12374": {
      "dom_id": "step-q12374",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are two events such that $P(A|B)=0.6,$ $P(A)=0.6,$ $P(B)=0.8.$ Find $P(B|A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Use the multiplication law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap B) & =P(B)\\cdot P(A|B) \\\\  & =0.8\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(B|A),$ we use \n\n$$\nP(B|A)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|A) & =\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.48}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
