    "q-88601": {
      "dom_id": "step-q88601",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Two cards are randomly drawn from a pack of $52$ cards one by one without replacement. What is the probability that both the cards are diamonds?",
      "worked_solution": "Let us call ${D}_{1}$ the event \"the first card drawn is a diamond\" and ${D}_{2}$ the event \"the second card drawn is a diamond.\" \n\nThe required probability is $P({D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}),$ which is given by\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2})=P({D}_{1})\\cdot P({D}_{2}|{D}_{1}).\n$$\n\nLet's find $P({D}_{1})$ and $P({D}_{2}|{D}_{1}).$\n\n- There are $13$ diamonds among the $52$ cards, so we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})=\\frac{13}{52}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- If the first card drawn is a diamond, there are $12$  diamonds left in the pack among the remaining $51$ cards. So, we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{2}|{D}_{1})=\\frac{12}{51}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above data into the multiplication law for conditional probability, we get\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2})=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{12}{51}=\\frac{1}{17}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
